Added conducts need added fields to u_uconduct, which is a part of the you
struct, so they WILL invalidate existing save and bones files.
Even if you put conducts somewhere else instead of struct you, they must
be in the save file. (Unless you add another file that is accessed
together with the save file, but I don't think it's worthwhile anyway..)

BTW, I examined the saving of bones files and it looked to me that the
struct you is not saved there, so I tried to tweak src/version.c to ignore
the difference in the size of 'you' when loading bones (but not when
loading save files). I had some OS/2 bones (compiled with gcc/emx with the
omf options) where sizeof(you) is 452 and tried loading them in tweaked
win32 binaries (compiled with the borland command line tools) where
sizeof(you) is 456 and everything else in the magic numbers match. 
The bones file I tried got recognized and the game tried to load it, but
gave a program error window saying  "NetHack.exe has generated errors and
will be closed by Windows. You will need to restart the program. An error
log is being created.".
But I did not find any error log created at that time anywhere. I looked
at the directory where I run the binaries, the bones and save directories
under it, the root directory and tmp..
Well, maybe the different size of struct you is not the only difference
between OS/2 and win32 bones, just the only one detected by the magic
numbers.
If somebody is interested in what I tried to do even though it didn't work
as I expected, I could extract a diff.

